description:
The pattern:

  match backend_result {
      Ok(...) => { /* success */ }
      Err(BackendSetError::SetError(set_err)) => { ... insert(set_error_value(&set_err)); }
      Err(BackendSetError::Other(e)) => { ... insert(json!({type: serverFail, description: e})); }
      Err(_) => { ... insert(json!({type: serverFail, description: 'unhandled backend error variant'})); }
  }

appears in task.rs create (172-199), task.rs update (289-321), task.rs destroy (343-369), task.rs copy (455-482), task_list.rs create (131-161), task_list.rs update (186-220), task_list.rs destroy (251-277), task_notification.rs destroy (132-158). That is eight near-identical 30-line blocks. The Err(_) catch-all is doubly wrong: BackendSetError is #[non_exhaustive], and any future variant added without sweep-updating all eight call sites silently maps to 'unhandled backend error variant' — a serverFail with no useful description rather than a structured SetError.

Suggested rewrite: a private helper

  fn map_backend_error<E: Display>(err: BackendSetError<E>) -> serde_json::Value {
      match err {
          BackendSetError::SetError(e) => set_error_value(&e),
          BackendSetError::Other(e) => json!({type: serverFail, description: e.to_string()}),
          _ => json!({type: serverFail, description: 'unhandled backend error variant'}),
      }
  }

reduces each match arm to one line:

  Err(e) => { not_xxx.insert(id, map_backend_error(e)); }

This is a cookie-cutter pattern across multiple extension-server crates per the canonical-template rule. Recommend filing the consolidation as a workspace sweep so it lands on jmap-mail-server (canonical) first and then propagates to chat/calendars/tasks/contacts/filenode/metadata/sharing in one pass.
